      },
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I'll add a +1 for a Native Linux Build, BUT having skimmed over a lot of the comments, I Have to laugh about a lot of it because so many comments are completely misunderstanding the complexity of what was already state by Tupper. Better linux support and considerations is already more than we can ask for. (Selfie cam fix when?) \n\nTL;DR Getting VRChat to run fully under proton is actually the better long term solution, DXVK Under Proton already does an amazing job of translating DX12 shaders to Vulkan and while A Native client would be amazing, the complexity of it does track. \n\n___ Rant ___\n\nYou can't \"Just shim DXVK\" in to the unity engine.\n\nThe shaders are compiled to binary form for Windows, \n\n- Mobile versions of the avatars use pre-defined potato shaders, that's why Mobile avis always have this hard limit on materials. \n\nRunning VRChat under Proton works just fine, if you can't follow a simple guide on how to extract proton-GE-x-rtsp in to your steam's compatabilitytools.d folder then that's your skill issue. I'm sorry, but moving a file from folder A to folder B is one of the most basic of computer skills, there's no excuse if a guide tells you exactly where to place it.\n\nIF you're using a broken compositor that can't render/pace frames properly, that's also a you problem - use software that doesn't suck (I'm calling out the ALVR and Steam VR combo for this one, it sucks right now i'm Sorry.)",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
